"# Authentication Architecture\n\n## Overview\n\nThe authentication layer uses JWTs issued by the internal auth service. Tokens are short-lived (15 minutes) with a sliding refresh window of 7 days.\n\n## Components\n\n| Component | Responsibility |\n|-----------|----------------|\n| `auth-service` | Token issuance and refresh |\n| `pkg/jwt` | Signing and verification |\n| `pkg/blocklist` | Revoked token store (Redis) |\n\n## Token lifecycle\n\n1. Client POSTs credentials to `/auth/token`.\n2. Service validates credentials and returns `{access_token, refresh_token, expires_in}`.\n3. Client attaches `Authorization: Bearer <access_token>` to all API requests.\n4. On 401, client uses `refresh_token` to obtain a new access token.\n5. Logout calls `/auth/revoke` which adds the token ID to the blocklist.\n\n## Security notes\n\n- Tokens are signed HS256 using the `JWT_SECRET` env var sourced from the secrets manager.\n- The blocklist TTL matches the token max expiry to bound Redis storage.\n"
